<BaseLayout
  title="Hotel &amp; Motel Pest Control | West Texas"
  description="Bed bug detection and treatment for lodging on the I-20 corridor and across the Permian Basin, with the notice rule that names hotels specifically."
  path="/commercial/hotels/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <Crumbs items={crumbs} />

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Commercial · hotels &amp; motels</p>
      <h1>Hotels and motels</h1>
      <div class="measure"><AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} /></div>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Lodging is the sector where a single pest becomes an existential business problem, and where the
          industry's instincts about that pest are almost entirely wrong.
        </p>
        <p>
          The instinct is to treat bed bugs as a housekeeping failure — something that happens to badly run
          properties. That belief is why staff hide what they find, why introductions run for weeks before
          anybody reports them, and why a contained problem becomes a floor. This page starts there.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Introduction, not sanitation</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Bed bugs arrive in luggage.</strong> In clothing, in a bag set on a bed, in second-hand
          furniture, in a delivery. They are not attracted by dirt, they do not breed in it, and their
          presence says nothing whatsoever about how a property is cleaned.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Which means a property that takes travellers is exposed by definition.</strong> The rate
          of introduction is a function of throughput, not of standards. Expensive hotels get them.
          Hospitals get them. The cleanest property on the interstate gets them.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The only thing housekeeping changes is detection.</strong> A tidy room with minimal
          clutter is easier to inspect, and an introduction is found earlier. That is a real and valuable
          effect, and it is entirely different from causation.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why this framing matters operationally.</strong> If your staff believe finding bed bugs
          reflects on their work, they will not report them. They will strip the room, wash everything on
          hot, say nothing, and hope. That delay is what converts a single introduced room into a
          multi-room problem, and it is caused by the culture rather than by the insect.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The fix is to make reporting safe and routine.</strong> A finding is good news — it means
          the system worked. Tell your team that explicitly, and mean it, because the alternative costs far
          more than any treatment.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Detection at turnover is the whole game</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Every hotel has a moment, several times a week per room, when the bed is stripped and the room is
          empty. That is a free inspection opportunity that most properties do not use.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What to look at, in order.</strong> Mattress seams and the tags first. Then the box
          spring — including underneath the fabric at the corners, which is the most productive single spot
          in a hotel room and the one nobody checks. Then the frame, particularly anywhere two pieces bolt
          together. Then the headboard, and behind it if it is wall-mounted.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Then the fifteen-foot radius around the sleeping position.</strong> Nightstands, the
          seams of upholstered chairs, the edge of carpet where it meets the wall, the joint of a
          baseboard, behind a picture frame.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What you are looking for is evidence, not insects.</strong> Small dark spots — digested
          blood — along seams and joints. Shed skins that look like translucent empty shells. Live insects
          roughly the size and shape of an apple seed, flattened, reddish-brown and faster than people
          expect.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Not bites.</strong> People react enormously differently — some develop pronounced welts,
          some show nothing at all, and two people in the same bed can have entirely different experiences.
          A guest complaint about bites is a reason to inspect, not a diagnosis.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Encasements make this dramatically easier.</strong> A mattress and box spring encasement
          removes the most complicated harbourage in the room from the equation and forces anything present
          onto the outside where it can be seen. They are inexpensive, they are permanent, and on a
          property with any history they pay for themselves in inspection time alone.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Reduce the crevices near the bed.</strong> Not for appearance — for detection. Every
          additional harbourage point within fifteen feet of where somebody sleeps is somewhere an
          introduction can establish unseen.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Photograph anything you find</strong> and text it before we come out. It resolves in ten
          seconds what a description cannot, and it means the visit arrives knowing what it is dealing
          with.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What treatment actually involves</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>The inspection comes first and takes longer than you expect.</strong> Beds apart,
          mattresses lifted, box spring corners opened, frames checked at the joints, headboards off the
          wall. Then the radius around each sleeping position. On a hotel this also means the adjacent
          rooms, because scope is the first question.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Then a conversation about scope and cost before anything is applied.</strong> If the
          inspection changes the picture — and on lodging it frequently does, because infestations are
          rarely confined to the room that prompted the call — the revised number is discussed on the spot
          rather than appearing on an invoice.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Then conventional chemical treatment</strong>, placed according to the harbourage found.
          The insect growth regulator matters as much as the adulticide here, because it addresses the part
          of the population that is not currently a reproducing adult — which is the part a straight
          adulticide misses entirely.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Then the follow-up, on the date agreed.</strong> This is not optional and it is not
          padding. A conventional treatment deals effectively with the insects present when it is applied
          and does considerably less to eggs. Those hatch on their own schedule, producing a new generation
          that arrives days or weeks later and feels exactly like a treatment failure. It is not. It is the
          reason the second visit exists, and the interval is set by the biology rather than by
          convenience.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Plan the room out of inventory for the sequence</strong>, not for a single afternoon.
          That is the honest operational answer and it is better to know it when you book than to discover
          it when you are trying to sell the room.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What we do not do.</strong> No heat treatment — thermal remediation needs equipment we do
          not carry. No fumigation. No promise of single-visit resolution. Where heat is genuinely right
          for a property, you need a provider who does it and you will be told so.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Stopping it spreading</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Most multi-room bed bug problems in lodging were single-room problems that got moved.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Do not carry the mattress out through the corridor.</strong> The instinct to remove and
          replace is strong and it is how a contained problem is distributed along a floor. If a mattress
          does need to go, it goes after treatment and wrapped.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Do not relocate a guest's belongings unbagged.</strong> Moving a guest is often the right
          call for the guest; moving their luggage into another room without containing it moves the
          problem with them.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Do not move staff equipment between rooms without thought.</strong> Housekeeping carts,
          linen bags and vacuum cleaners all travel the floor, and a bag that sat on an infested bed goes
          everywhere the cart goes.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Inspect the neighbours before deciding scope.</strong> Either side, above and below. Bed
          bugs move between rooms through wall voids, plumbing chases and electrical penetrations, and the
          room that reported is not necessarily the room with the source. Treating one room while an
          adjacent one is untreated is frequently money spent against continuous re-supply.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Keep a room history.</strong> Which rooms have had findings, when, and what was done. On
          a property of any size, patterns emerge — a particular floor, a particular corridor, rooms
          adjacent to a specific chase — and that pattern is worth more than any single inspection.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Take the room out of inventory properly.</strong> A room under treatment is not a room
          that can be sold on the second night because it looks fine. It looks fine before the follow-up
          too, and selling it in between is how a treated room becomes an untreated one with a new guest in
          it — and how a guest complaint arrives against a property that had already found and addressed
          the problem.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And handle the guest conversation separately from the pest one.</strong> That is a
          hospitality decision and it is yours. What we can do is give you an accurate account of what was
          found and what has been done, which is a considerably better footing for that
          conversation than uncertainty would be.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Building a detection routine that staff will actually use</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          A programme that depends on housekeeping doing a careful ten-minute inspection of every room
          every day will not survive contact with a real property. One that fits inside what they already
          do might.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Attach it to the strip, not to a separate task.</strong> The bed is already being
          stripped. The thirty seconds of looking at the mattress seam and the box spring corners while the
          linen is off costs nothing extra, and it covers the two most productive locations in the room.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Give people a card, not a lecture.</strong> A laminated card at the cart with the three
          things to look for — dark spots along seams, translucent shed skins, an apple-seed-shaped insect
          — and a photograph of each does more than a training session. Recognition is visual and it is
          learned from pictures.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Make reporting a single, easy action</strong> that does not require judgement. The staff
          member's job is to report what they saw, not to decide whether it counts. Anybody being asked to
          assess severity will resolve doubt by not reporting.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Never penalise a finding.</strong> If a report creates work, blame or suspicion for the
          person who made it, you have built a system that suppresses information. This is the single most
          common failure in lodging pest management and it is entirely cultural.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Prioritise the rooms most likely to have something.</strong> Rooms that have had a
          finding before. Rooms adjacent to those. Rooms with high turnover or long stays. Rooms nearest
          the entrance, the laundry, or a shared chase.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And write down what was found and where.</strong> A room history is worth more than any
          single inspection, because it is where patterns show up — and patterns are what tell you whether
          you have a series of introductions or one problem moving.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The rest of the building</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Bed bugs get the attention, and they are not the only thing a lodging property deals with.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Large roaches from drains.</strong> A reddish-brown roach an inch and a half to two
          inches long, appearing in a bathroom or a corridor at night, is almost certainly an American
          cockroach — an outdoor and sewer insect that AgriLife describes as one of the most common
          cockroaches in sewer systems. It came in through a floor drain, a door threshold or a roof
          soffit. The fix is plumbing and exclusion, not a treatment programme.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The cheapest intervention available:</strong> keep P-traps filled. AgriLife's guidance is
          verbatim — keep P-traps filled with water to create a barrier between the sewer and the building.
          In a hotel the drains that dry out are in rooms that have been out of inventory, in unused
          housekeeping closets, and in seasonal areas. Running water through them on a schedule closes the
          route entirely.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Check door thresholds and roof soffits</strong>, which are the other two entry routes
          AgriLife names specifically. On a motel layout, where a good share of the doors open directly to
          the outside and take constant use, the sweeps wear out faster than anybody replaces them, and
          that gap is doing more work against you than any single treatment can undo.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Rodents in back-of-house.</strong> Laundry, storage, kitchen and loading areas. The
          exclusion standard is a quarter of an inch, sealed with metal or concrete rather than foam or
          plastic, and stored material kept eighteen inches off the floor and away from walls so runways
          can be seen.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Scorpions, in this region specifically.</strong> Ground-floor rooms, exterior corridors
          and anything with a door opening directly outside. AgriLife's guidance on entry — under doors and
          through small cracks, seeking cooler temperatures — applies exactly to a motel layout, and door
          sweeps are the single highest-value item.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And on the I-20 corridor specifically</strong>, throughput is the variable that drives
          all of it. Stanton sits directly on the interstate between Midland and Big Spring, and any
          premises serving a passing population sees a rate of introduction that a residential building
          does not.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Paperwork, notice and how we work with you</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>The notice rule names you.</strong> Rule 4 TAC §7.146(c) lists hotels, motels and lodges
          among the premises where we must provide a pest control sign at least forty-eight hours before a
          planned indoor treatment, to the chief administrator, IPM coordinator or building manager. Under
          §7.148(b) that person posts it in an area of common access at least forty-eight hours ahead and
          makes the consumer information sheet available to anyone working or residing in the building on
          request.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And §7.146(d) gives you room to work.</strong> No violation where the space is vacant,
          unused and unoccupied — which for a hotel is most rooms most of the time. Treating out of an
          unoccupied block simplifies the notice mechanics as well as the operational ones, and it is worth
          scheduling around deliberately.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Records for two years.</strong> Under §7.144 we keep pest control use records including
          the pesticides used with their EPA registration numbers, amounts, mixing rates, target pest,
          dates and applicator details. On lodging that record matters for more than compliance — it is
          what you produce if a guest claim ever arises, and it is the difference between a documented
          response and a recollection.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Certificate of insurance</strong> naming your entity, on request, and worth arranging
          before the first visit.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Discretion.</strong> No branded wrap announcing what is being treated, and we do not
          discuss your property with anyone not authorised on the job. On lodging that is not a nicety, it is part of the service.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Scheduling around occupancy.</strong> Out of hours, out of an unoccupied block, or during
          a low-occupancy window — whichever suits. Tell us your pattern rather than calling when a room is
          already out of service.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And Spanish.</strong> Valentin speaks it fluently and explains preparation to
          housekeeping staff directly, without an interpreter. On a property where the people most likely
          to find an introduction first are the people cleaning the rooms, that is worth a great deal more
          than it sounds.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqs} heading="Hotel pest control questions" />
  <Cta
    line="Tell him how many rooms, whether the finding is in one room or several, and whether the rooms either side have been looked at. Scope is the first question and it decides everything else."
  />
</BaseLayout>
